1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hulagu Khan, also known as Hülegü, Hulegu or Halaku (Mongolian: Хүлэгү, derived from the word for "surplus"[1]), Khülegü; Chagatai/Urdu/Persian: ہلاکو - Hulaku; Arabic: هولاكو‎;Chinese: 旭烈兀; c. 1217 – 8 February 12
2 CONC 65), was a Mongol ruler who conquered much of Southwest Asia. Son of Tolui and the Kerait princess Sorghaghtani Beki, he was a grandson of Genghis Khan...
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hulagu_Khan
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hulagu_Khan Wikipedia] 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikibedia.ru/wiki/Hulagu_Khan
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Read: Early Mongol Rule in 13th century by George Lane
2 CONT https://bit.ly/3jQhwq9
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT "If Chinggiz Khan had been the punishment of God, his grand son Hulegu was God's secret intent revealed" p. 257 The Mongols in Iran 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Abu Jaffar Mohammad Allakbar ben Hassan" 
2 CONT
2 CONT 40 ans apres la defaite et la fuite de Soltan Mohammad Kharazmshah, l'armee mongole revient en Iran sous la direction de Hulagu Khan.
2 CONT
2 CONT la mere de Hulagu Sorghatani Bigui etait chretienne ainsi que sa femme Doquz Khatoun  qui etait de religion nestorienne. Ceux qui aiderent Hulagu dans ce combat etaient d'une part des Mongols musulmans qui voulaient detruire les I
2 CONC smailiens et d'autre part des Armeniens qui pour des raisons de differents religieux avec les musulmans voulaient se debarrasser du Caliphat Abbasside et voulaient que Hulagu prenne Baghdad et mette egalement fin aux musulmans d'E
2 CONC gypte (fatimides) que les croises combattaient. C'est pourquoi un grand nombre des combattants de l'armee de Hulagu etaient des mongols convertis au christianisme. 
2 CONT
2 CONT End of the Caliphate era. [... which marked the Caliphate for an extinction which would come from Genghis' grandson, Hulegu.] ref http://bit.ly/248wuZW
2 CONT
2 CONT HULAGU ([1217]-Jaghatu 19 Feb 1265).  Abul-Ghazi Bahadur names “Mangou-Qaan, Holagou-Khan, Qoubilai-Qaan et Mouga-Oghoul” as the sons of “Touli Khan” by “Sourqouqti-Bigui”[304].  He supervised the extension of the Mongol Empire in
2 CONC to the Near East the campaign into Persia starting in 1253.  His forces annihilated the Assassins in Persia in 1257, captured Baghdad in 1258 and entered Syria in 1259[305].  His brother Kubilai gave him the hereditary government 
2 CONC of the Mongol possessions in south-western Asia and the title HULAGU Il-Khan
2 CONT
2 CONT [...Hulagu Khan reached Khorasan and Iran in 651 A.H. The rulers of Azarbaijan, Shirvan (part of modern day Azarbaijan) and Girjistan presented themselves befor Hulagu Khan and offered their allegiance to him. ARGUN AGA OBRAT proc
2 CONC eeded from Khorasan to meet Mungku Khan {Mongke} ...]...[ Alamut fort fell in 656 A.H. and Ismailite ruler Shah Ruknuddin Khurshaw was arrested and produced before Hulagu Khan who sent him to Mungku Khan in Karakorum with the inst
2 CONC ructions that he should be done away with on the way...]  ref  page 312 The History of Islam, Volume 2 By Akbar Shāh K̲h̲ān Najībābādī http://bit.ly/1RiJ7N8
2 CONT
2 CONT Protector of Christians
2 CONT
2 CONT  هولاکو با فتح ایران سلسله ایلخانیان ایران و قوبیلای با فتح چین سلسله یوان چین را بنیان نهادند Lui et ses descendants regnent sur les Ilkhanates d'Iran pendant 8 ans. Sa mere ainsi que son epouse sont chretiennes
2 CONT
2 CONT "... the kingdom of Hulacu Can, residing in Azarbaijana and Irac-Arabi" ref: p.334 The History of Timur-Bec: Known by the Name of Tamerlain the Great ..., Volume 2 By Sharaf al-Dīn 'Alī (Yazdī.), John Darby
2 CONT
2 CONT [Favored summer resi­dences included Ālātāḡ (Ala Dağ, northeast of Lake Van), where Hülegü built a palace (Rašīd-al-Dīn, Jāmeʿ al-tawārīḵ III, Baku, p. 90), Tabrīz, and Qarābāḡ. All these locations lay within a relatively restrict
2 CONC ed area in northwestern Persia, as dictated by the military threat from the Mongols of the Golden Horde in the Caucasus. But occasionally the il-khan wintered in Baghdad, in the so-called “palace of the catholicus,” according to ʿ
2 CONC Omarī (pp. 91-92); and Abaqa and Öljeitü (Ūljāytū) also sometimes wintered in Māzandarān.] ref: http://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/courts-and-courtiers-iv
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: October 15, 1218 Died: February 8, 1265, Maragheh, Iran Buried: Lake Urmia, Iran Spouse: Doquz Khatun (m. ?–1265) Siblings: Kublai Khan, Möngke Khan Parents: Sorghaghtani Beki, Tolui
2 CONT
2 CONT Commence la dynastie des ILKHANATES en Iran - 1er Ilkhan Il met fin a la dynastie des Abbasides (Khawrazmshah) Il se debarasse du califat des Ismailis et de Baghdad Il prend la forteresse de Alamut (Hassan Sabba, le vieil homme de
2 CONC  la montagne) et envoie Shah Rukneddin Khurshaw dans le Karakorum a son frere Mungku pour le mettre a mort. Toutefois, Khawaja Nasirrudin Tusi qui est lui-meme un Ismailien et travaillait a la cour de Shah Rukneddin est nomme cour
2 CONC tier a la cour de Hulagu Khan
2 CONT
2 CONT Il recoit allegeance de  Atabak Said Ben Abu Bakr, le dirigeant de la Perse, de Badruddin Lulu, le dirigeant de Mosoul et du sultan Azizuddin Suljuk, le dirigeant du sultanat Seljuk de Rome (aujourd'hui la Turquie).
2 CONT
2 CONT A fait construire un planetarieum avec l'aide de Nasirrudin Tusi et des scientifiques, a Maragheh.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il nomme Shamsuddin Mohamad Jawini son ministre.  http://bit.ly/1RiJ7N8
2 CONT
2 CONT Hulagu Khan, also known as Hülegü or Hulegu (Mongolian: Хүлэгу хаан; Chagatai: ہلاکو; Persian: هولاکو خان‎‎; Chinese: 旭烈兀; c. 1218 – 8 February 1265), was a Mongol ruler who conquered much of Western Asia. Son of Tolui and the Khe
2 CONC reid princess Sorghaghtani Beki, he was a grandson of Genghis Khan and brother of Ariq Böke, Möngke Khan, and Kublai Khan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hulagu's army greatly expanded the southwestern portion of the Mongol Empire, founding the Ilkhanate of Persia, a precursor to the eventual Safavid dynasty, and then the modern state of Iran. Under Hulagu's leadership, the siege o
2 CONC f Baghdad (1258) destroyed the greatest center of Islamic power and also weakened Damascus, causing a shift of Islamic influence to the Mamluk Sultanate in Cairo. Under Hulagu's dynasty, Iranian historians began writing in Persian
2 CONC  rather than Arabic.
2 CONT
2 CONT Genghis' descendants intermarried frequently. For instance, the Jochids took wives from the Ilkhan dynasty of Persia, whose progenitor was Hulagu Khan. As a consequence, it is likely that many Jochids had other sons of Genghis Kha
2 CONC n among their maternal ancestors.
2 CONT
2 CONT ref: Genghis Khan and Monghol rule by George Lane  http://bit.ly/1Q0Llhs "... Hulgeu established his capital in the verdant town of MARAGHEH not far from the city of Tabriz........ Hulegu died in Maragheh in 1265 ..." as of page 6
2 CONC 3
2 CONT
2 CONT page 252 The Mongols in Iran 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Fifth son of Tolui
2 CONT
2 CONT Hulegu was sent in 1255 by his brother Munke (who was the Great Khan from 1251 to 1258 ), to destroy what remained of the Member Muslims in Southwest Asia. The first to fall under his blows was the Luristan , in the south of Iran 
2 CONC ; the second to be destroyed was the statelet constituted by the sect Ismaili of Assassins ; the third was to be destroyed the same Caliphate Abbasid . Then came the turn of the emirates Ayyubids in Syria and, finally, was plannin
2 CONC g to make the submission or destruction of the Sultanate Mamluk d ' Egypt .
2 CONT
2 CONT Assassins and the march on Baghdad
2 CONT
2 CONT The Javanshir tribe joined the 120 000 men army of Hulagu khan. (page 129 of the Caucasus and Globalization in documents)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===
