2 CONT https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%9A%D1%83%D1%87%D0%BB%D1%83%D0%BA Кучлук , Кучулук, Кушлук (монг. Хүчлүг) (? — 1218) — сын найманского хана Таяна. После разгрома найманов войсками Тэмуджина и гибели Таяна (1204) Кучлук ушёл на за
2 CONC пад, в иртышские степи, где объединился с ханом меркитов Тохтоа-беки, также бежавшим от монголов. Но в 1205 году[1] (или 1208)[2] Кучлук и Тохтоа были разбиты монголами в долине Иртыша. Кучлуку вновь удалось бежать, на этот раз — 
2 CONC в Семиречье, к гурхану кара-киданей Елюй Чжулху. Гурхан милостиво принял Кучлука и выдал за него свою дочь. Тем не менее, когда хорезмшах Мухаммед отнял у кара-киданей Самарканд и Бухару, Кучлук не пришёл на помощь гурхану, захват
2 CONC ил его казну и попытался взять в плен самого Чжулху. Однако в сражении близ Баласагуна Кучлук был разбит. Войско гурхана отбило казну, но не вернуло её правителю. В результате этого бунта гурхан был захвачен Кучлуком (1211). Чжулх
2 CONC у был оставлен его титул, но реальной властью в стране обладал Кучлук. В 1213 году Чжулху умер, а Кучлук был провозглашён гурханом. Кучлук, бывший сначала, видимо, несторианином, а затем начавший под влиянием жены «поклоняться стр
2 CONC анным богам»,[3] устроил религиозные гонения на мусульман, составлявших большинство населения государства. В 1218 году Кучлук осадил город Алмалык, правитель которого признавал власть монголов. В ответ Чингисхан отправил против Ку
2 CONC члука 20-тысячное войско Джэбэ-нойона. При вступлении в кара-киданьское государство Джэбэ провозгласил религиозную свободу, и мусульмане стали убивать воинов Кучлука, находившихся на постое в городах. Кучлук бежал, но был пойман в
2 CONC  горах Памира и обезглавлен.
2 CONT
2 CONT В современной культуре:
2 CONT
2 CONT В кинематографе: Кучлук фигурирует в китайско-монгольском 30-серийном сериале «Чингисхан» (серии 21-24). «Тайна Чингис Хаана» — кинофильм 2009 года, в роли Кучлук играет Олег Тактаров.
2 CONT
2 CONT В документальном кино: Тайны древности. Варвары. Часть 2. Монголы (США; 2003).
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kuchlug Kuchlug (also spelled Küchlüg, Küçlüg, Güčülüg) was a member of the Naiman tribe of western Mongolia who became the last ruler of Qara Khitai empire. The Naimans were defeated by Genghis Khan 
2 CONC and he fled westward to the Qara Khitai, where he became an advisor. He later rebelled, usurped the throne and took control of Qara Khitai. He was killed in 1218 by the Mongols and the domain of the Qara Khitai absorbed into the r
2 CONC ising Mongol Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Naiman Origins and Westward Flight Kuchlug was the son of Taibuqa, the Tayang Khan (leader) of the Naimans, a Mongol-speaking tribe. In 1204, Jamuqa, the chief Mongol rival of Temüjin (later known as Genghis Khan), fled to the Nai
2 CONC mans. Temüjin followed and launched an assault upon the tribe. Taibuqa at first hesitated, considering it better to fall back to the Altai Mountains and attack the Mongols from there. Kuchlug, however, favored a direct assault on 
2 CONC the Mongols on open ground. He went so far as to dismiss his father's plan as cowardly. Taibuqa relented and allowed Kuchlug to carry out his attack.
2 CONT
2 CONT The battle was a disaster for the Naiman. Jamuqa abandoned them and fled. Taibuqa was mortally injured, and his main commander was killed. The rest of the tribe surrendered to Temüjin and were absorbed into his ranks. Kuchlug mana
2 CONC ged to escape and with a few Naiman soldiers fled westward towards the Kara Irtish.
2 CONT
2 CONT Afterwards Genghis Khan was wary of the threat Kuchlug still posed. In 1208, they again met in battle, and Kuchlug was pushed further west into Semirechye. Reeling from a second defeat at the hands of the Mongols, Kuchlug turned t
2 CONC o the Qara Khitai in Balasaghun for protection.
2 CONT
2 CONT Qara Khitai Empire Kuchlug was welcomed by Zhilugu, the Gur-khan (meaning Universal Khan), ruler of the Qara Khitai. Once he was accepted into the empire, Kuchlug quickly gained a strong foothold. He found service as an advisor fo
2 CONC r Gur-khan, and was allowed to marry one of the Gur-khan's daughters, Qûngû. He was later given the title of Khan, and was allowed to reorganize his fellow Naimans into a military unit under his command.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Usurpation of the throne Around that time, the Qara Khitai were dealing with rebellions in the east, as well as engaging in a struggle against Muhammad II of the Khwarezmian Empire in the west. The Khwarezm-Shah took Bukhara in 12
2 CONC 07, but was defeated by the Qara Khitai at Samarkand. Kuchlug, however, apparently had formed an alliance with the Khwarezm-Shah. In 1210, while the Gur-khan was dealing with a revolt by the Karakhanids at Samarkand, Kuchlug took 
2 CONC the chance to rebel against his father-in-law, seizing the Qara Khitai's treasury at Uzgen. The Gur-khan left Samarkand to deal with Kuchlug, but the Khwarezm-Shah Muhammad used the opportunity to seize Samarkand, then defeated th
2 CONC e Qara Khitai near Talas and gained control of Transoxiana.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Gur-khan pulled back to his capital of Balasagun, and defeated Kuchlug who retreated eastward to his Naiman realm. However, in 1211, while the Gur-khan was out hunting, he was ambushed and captured by Kuchlug. The Khwarezm-Sha
2 CONC h then joined Kuchlug in capturing the Qara Khitai Empire. The Gur-khan was allowed to remain ruler of the Qara Khitai, at least in name, but Kuchlug retained the real power.[2] When the Gur-khan died in 1213, Kuchlug took direct 
2 CONC control of the empire. While Kuchlug likely only intended to usurp the throne of the Qara Khitai, many historians considered the death of the Gur-khan the end of the Qara Khitai Empire.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Kuchlug's rule
2 CONT
2 CONT Khwarezmid Empire Conflicts with Muhammad II Once Kuchlug had established himself as ruler, Muhammad demanded the Qara Khitai Gur-khan to be handed over and a princess as a reward for his previous support. Kuchlug tried to stall, 
2 CONC but eventually Muhammad became more aggressive. However, when Kuchlug threatened to resolve the rivalry by direct combat, Muhammad chose to evacuate the region of upper Jaxartes (Syr-Darya), demolished the settlements there in an 
2 CONC attempt to form a buffer zone between his empire and Kuchlug's,[4] and the Syr-Darya eventually came to be the de facto border between the two rulers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Religious policy Kuchlug was from the Naiman tribe, which were Nestorians. His wife, daughter of the Gur-khan, was a Buddhist, and convinced him to adopt Buddhism. However, in contrast to the policy of religious tolerance of the p
2 CONC revious Qara Khitai ruler Gur-khans, once Kuchlug assumed power, he was reported to have instituted anti-Muslim policies. According to Persian historian Ata-Malik Juvayni, he would demand that a town’s Muslim population make a cho
2 CONC ice between either converting to Nestorianism or Buddhism, or donning Khitan garment, of which the population would choose to wear Khitan clothing.[5] He was also reported to have crucified the imam of Hotan onto the door of his m
2 CONC adrassa.[6] His anti-Muslim policies and struggle against Muhammad may have served to inspire the legend of another incarnation of the Christian king of the Far East Prester John.
2 CONT
2 CONT Downfall and death Kuchlug attacked the city of Almaliq, and the Karlugs there appealed to Genghis Khan for help.[7] In 1216, Genghis Khan dispatched his general Jebe to pursue Kuchlug. The Mongol first went to Almaliq, then proce
2 CONC eded on to the capital city of Balasaghun near which they defeated a Qara Khitai force of 30,000 men. Kuchlug fled southwards to Kashgar, however, his previous acts of pillaging and burning harvests in Kashgar when he first captur
2 CONC ed the town, his anti-Muslim policies, as well as the billeting of his troops on local households, had antagonized the people of Kashgar.[8] When the Mongols approached Kashgar, Kuchlug, unable to find support in Kashgar, fled aga
2 CONC in. According to Ata-Malik Juvayni, the people of Kashgar, then killed his soldiers. He continued south across the Pamirs, eventually reaching the border between Badakhshan and Wakhan in 1218.[9] There, a group of hunters caught h
2 CONC im and handed him over to the Mongols.[10] Kuchlug was beheaded, and according to the Chinese historical work Yuan Shi, his head was displayed across his former realm.[11]
