1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nogai_Khan
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Nogai''' (died 1299/1300) or '''Noğay''' (pronouned no-guy, no-khay), also spelled '''Nogay, Nohai, Nokhai, Noqai''',Ngoche, Noche, '''Kara Nokhai''', and '''Isa Nogai''', was a general and kingmaker[4] of the Golden Horde and 
2 CONC a great-great-grandson of Genghis Khan. His grandfather was Bo'al/Baul/Teval Khan, the 7th son of Jochi. Nogai Khan was also a notable convert to Islam.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though he never formally ruled the Golden Horde himself, he was effectively the co-ruler of the state alongside whatever khan was in power at the time, and had unrestricted control over the portions west of the Dnieper. At his hei
2 CONC ght, Nogai was one of the most powerful men in Europe, and widely thought of as the Horde's true head. The Russian chroniclers gave him the title of tsar, and the Franciscan missionaries in the Crimea spoke of him as a co-emperor.
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT #	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nogai_Khan#Name Name]
2 CONT #	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nogai_Khan#Early_life_under_Batu_and_Berke Early life under Batu and Berke]
2 CONT #	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nogai_Khan#Rise_to_power_in_Golden_Horde_and_Europe:_1262%E2%80%931266 Rise to power in Golden Horde and Europe: 1262–1266]
2 CONT #	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nogai_Khan#De_facto_rule:_1266%E2%80%931294 De facto rule: 1266–1294]
2 CONT #	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nogai_Khan#Conflict_with_Tokhta_and_death:_1294%E2%80%931300 Conflict with Tokhta and death: 1294–1300]
2 CONT #	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nogai_Khan#Personality_and_character Personality and character]
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MONGOLS.htm#NogaiKhandied1299
2 CONT
2 CONT i)          NOGAI Khan (-killed in battle on the Bug 1299).  He allied himself with Emperor Michael VIII, whose illegitimate daughter he married.  He severely plundered Bulgaria in 1271, in response to a request from the emperor w
2 CONC ho was seeking to exact revenge on Konstantin Tih Tsar of Bulgaria who had raided Thrace[95].  After the death of Mengu-Timur Khan in 1281, Nogai became more powerful in the Golden Horde than the official khans, with whom his rela
2 CONC tions were inevitably strained[96].  He was killed in battle by Tokhta Khan[97].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (a) CHUBAI, daughter of ---.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (b) YATLAQ, daughter of ---.  She was killed by her stepson.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (c) (1266) EUPHROSYNE [Eirene] Palaiologina, illegitimate daughter of Emperor MIKHAEL VIII & his mistress ---.  Pachymeres records that "imperator aliam suam filiam…ex pellicle genitam…Euphrosynam" married "principi Noga",
2 CONC  in [1266] from the context[98].  Pachymeres refers to the marriage between "Michaele Auguste…filiæ Euphrosynes" and "Nogas"[99].  
2 CONT
2 CONT   Nogai Khan & his wife (a) had two children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT   Nogai Khan & his wife (b) had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------
