2 CONT The Salghurids of Fars (1148-1282) (Persian: اتابکان فارس 'Atābakān-e Fārs' or سلغُریان 'Salghoriān'), were a dynasty of Turkmen origin[1] that ruled Fars, first as vassals of the Seljuqs then for the Khwarazm Shahs in the 13th ce
2 CONC ntury. The Salghurids were established by Sunqur in 1148, who had profited from the rebellions during the reign of Seljuq sultan Mas'ud b. Muhammad. Later the Salghurids were able to solidify their position in southern Persia to t
2 CONC he point of campaigning against Kurds and involving themselves in the succession of the Kirman Seljuqs,[2] holding Seljuq sultan Malik-Shah III's son Mahmud as a possible claimant to the Seljuq throne.[3] They captured Isfahan in 
2 CONC 1203-4,[4] and later occupied Bahrain taken from the Uyunid dynasty in 1235.[5]
