2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mamai]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT MAMAI (-after 1380).  He was an autonomous warlord who established his own horde in the steppe to the west of the Volga.  Not of the royal blood of Jenghiz Khan, he could not rule in his own name but exercised power through a seri
2 CONC es of puppets Khans[188].  Dmitry Grand Prince of Vladimir, Prince of Moscow took advantage of the confusion which accompanied the succession disputes in the Khanate by refusing to pay tribute.  He defeated the forces of Mamai at 
2 CONC Vozha river in 1378, at the battle of Kulikov near the upper Don river in 1380
2 CONT
2 CONT Mamai had to exercise his authority through a succession of puppet khans but could not assume the title of khan himself because he was not of the Genghisid lineage
2 CONT
2 CONT [Abdallah (1362–1370), actual ruler was Mamai
2 CONT
2 CONT Murad (1362–1367), actual ruler was Mamai
2 CONT
2 CONT Aziz (1367–1369), actual ruler was Mamai
2 CONT
2 CONT Jani Beg II (1369–1370), actual ruler was Mamai
2 CONT
2 CONT Muhammad Bolak (1370–1379), actual ruler was Mamai
2 CONT
2 CONT Tulun Beg Khanum (as regent) (1370–1373), actual ruler was Mamai
2 CONT
2 CONT Aig Beg (1373–1376), actual ruler was Mamai
2 CONT
2 CONT Arab Shaykh (1376–1379), actual ruler was Mamai
2 CONT
2 CONT Kagan Beg (1375–1376), actual ruler was Mamai
2 CONT
2 CONT Ilbani (1373–1376), actual ruler was Mamai
2 CONT
2 CONT Hajji Cherkes (1375–1376), actual ruler was Mamai]
2 CONT ----------------------
2 CONT Mamai Aziz Khan 1367 Descended from the same Borjigin clan as Chingiz Khan, Mamai is a powerful military leader in the Golden Horde and also governor of the Crimean peninsula. During the khanate of Berdi Beg, Mamai had gained the 
2 CONC office of beylerbay, making him head of the supreme court along with giving him the offices of military general and minister of foreign affairs. Since then he has been playing power politics with the shifting allegiances and claim
2 CONC s for power. Now he supports the claim by Abdullah Khan to rule the Blue Horde and firmly establishes himself as the true power behind the weakened throne. His puppet khans are shown as insets.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1367 - 1380  Governor of Crimea. Military general & de facto Blue Horde ruler.
2 CONT
2 CONT Berdi Beg (or Berdibek) was Khan of the Golden Horde from 1357 till 1359, succeeding his father Jani Beg of whom Berdi Beg may have been behind the poisoning. His reign was marked by turmoil and political murders, and in 1359 he w
2 CONC as assassinated by his brother Qulpa, after which the Golden Horde fell apart. He had one unnamed daughter who was the wife of Mamai.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Viceroy of Crimea Mamai became governor, sometime in the second half of the 1350s, of a territory that would later become the Crimean Khanate . During the government of Berdi Beg (1357-1359) Mamai was appointed as beg beqlar ( bey
2 CONC lerbey ), a position that simultaneously combines the functions of general of the army, the minister of Foreign Affairs, and the head of the supreme court.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the assassination of Berdi Beg by Qulpa in 1359, Mamai associated himself with one of the coalitions formed to depose Qulpa. During that period of time the Golden Horde was in relative chaos; numerous regional governors were 
2 CONC striving to become the Khan. Qulpa eventually was killed by Nawruz Beg who was himself assassinated less than six months later. Mamai, who was not a direct descendant of Genghis Khan, played a key role in promoting and supporting 
2 CONC rival khans of the White Horde (western part of Golden Horde). In 1361 he supported Ğabdullah, son of Uzbeg Khan, who became Khan in Crimea. Upon the death of Ğabdullah in 1370, Mamai supported Bulak under regency of Tulun Beg Kha
2 CONC num, who only briefly enjoyed power before being replaced by his main opponent Urus in 1372. After being badly defeated by Russians under Dmitri Donskoi first in 1378 at the Battle of the Vozha River and then at the Battle of Kuli
2 CONC kovo (1380), Mamai began to assemble a large force to punish Dmitri. But he was defeated by Tokhtamysh on the banks of the Kalka River and was either executed by Tokhtamysh or assassinated by the Genoese after fleeing to Caffa.[1]
2 CONC  The death of Mamai paved the way for the next khan to reunify the Golden Horde.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy The memory of Mamai has endured for centuries. Contemporary Russian language has an expression "как Мамай прошёл" (kak Mamaj prošjol), which translates roughly to "it's as if Mamai came through here", used to describe an ut
2 CONC ter mess.
2 CONT
2 CONT The burial of Mamai was found by the native Crimean and famous Russian painter Ivan Aivazovsky. Mamai was buried in a village of Şeyh Mamay (today Aivazovske, Kirovske Raion).
2 CONT
2 CONT Purported descendants Khan Mamatsios or Mamatzios, Mamatsou,Mamatsu (Mamai-son) Macedonie was a supposed son of Mamai. One of his sons, Manzur Kiyat, purportedly escaped to Lithuania, and, serving Grand Prince Vytautas the Great, 
2 CONC received the title of Prince of Hlinsk with multiple estates around the modern city of Poltava (Ukraine). These legendary events could have taken place in the 15th century, although the first documented mention of the Glinski prin
2 CONC ces is made in 1437. Michael Glinski was the most illustrious member of the family: he studied at the German university, took part as a knight in the Italian Wars, was the most powerful man in Lithuania in the 16th century, but la
2 CONC ter rebelled and run away with his brothers to Muscovy and helped the Russians to retake the city of Smolensk. His niece Elena Glinskaya was married to Vasily III, Grand Prince of Moscow, and Ivan the Terrible was her son.
