1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Khutulun was born about 1260.[3] By 1280, her father Kaidu became the most powerful ruler of Central Asia, reigning in the realms from western Mongolia to Oxus, and from the Central Siberian Plateau to India.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marco Polo described Khutulun as a superb warrior, one who could ride into enemy ranks and snatch a captive as easily as a hawk snatches a chicken. She assisted her father in many battles, particularly against the Yuan Dynasty of 
2 CONC her cousin the Great Khan – Kublai (r. 1260–1294).
2 CONT
2 CONT Khutulun insisted that any man who wished to marry her must defeat her in wrestling. Winning horses from competitions and the wagers of would-be suitors, it is said that she gathered a herd numbering ten thousand.[3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources vary about her husband's identity. Some chronicles say her husband was a handsome man who failed to assassinate her father and was taken prisoner; others refer to him as Kaidu's companion from the Choros clan. Rashid al-Di
2 CONC n wrote that Khutulun fell in love with Ghazan, Mongol ruler in Persia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of all Kaidu's children, Khutulun was the favorite, and the one from whom he most sought advice and political support. According to some accounts, he tried to name her as his successor to the khanate before he died in 1301. Howeve
2 CONC r, his choice was declined due to her male relatives. When Kaidu died, Khutulun guarded his tomb with the assistance of her brother Orus. She was challenged by her other brothers including Chapar and relative Duwa because she resi
2 CONC sted their succession. She died in 1306.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khutulun
