1 NOTE {geni:about_me} N. (three generations (about 150 years) between Jafar Khan and Mohammad Berdi Beg https://www.geni.com/people/Mohammad-Berdi-Beg-Khan-of-the-Batu-Clan-Chengizid/6000000021012760387 ; [En 1352 Berdi Beg devient gouv
2 CONC erneur de Tabriz et la meme annee son pere qui etait gouverneur de Ghobchagh meurt. Berdi Beg prend sa place et, aimant l'endroit, s'y installe. Ses descendants sur 7 generations vivent a Tabriz, jusqu'a l'epoque de Jafar Khan. [ 
2 CONC ref: Vie de Mirza Ali Hajeb e Dowle par Etemad Saltaneh II]]
2 CONT ===
2 CONT Just as his brother Qubilai Khan was making the journey from steppe to sown in a united China, so Hülegü was treading a similar path in the west when he founded the Ilkhanate and successfully integrated his top commanders and thei
2 CONC r families with the leading families from Iran, many of whose youngsters grew up alongside the children of the Turco- Mongol elite. It was from these cosmopolitan ordus that a new elite with a broader vision and braver aspirations
2 CONC  was to emerge.
2 CONT
2 CONT ref: Persian Notables Who Underpinned the Ilkhanate by George Lane.
2 CONT ====
2 CONT La tribu Bayat (azerbaïdjanais: Bayat tayfası, turc: Bayat boyu, turkmène: Baýat taýpasy) est l'une des tribus Oghuz au Turkménistan, en Iran, en Turquie, en Azerbaïdjan, en Irak et en Syrie. Lorsque les Turcs Oghuz ont commencé à
2 CONC  migrer des steppes d'Aral vers le Khorasan aux XIe et XIIIe siècles, les Bayat se sont répandus dans toute la région. Ce sont des groupes sous-ethniques de Turkmènes, Turcs et Azerbaïdjanais. Les Bayats sont musulmans et parlent 
2 CONC un dialecte du sud de la langue azerbaïdjanaise en Azerbaïdjan et en Iran, ou leur propre dialecte turc en Turquie.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the traditions of the Turkic tribes, the origin of this tribe is traced to Bayat - the grandson of Oghuz-Khan. In the book of the Khan and historian of the Khiva Khanate, Abu al-Ghazi Bahadur, “Genealogy of the Turkmens,” the m
2 CONC eaning of the name of the tribe is given as “rich”. Bayat was the second son of Gun-Khan, who was the first son of Oguz-Khan.[7] According to the work of the historian, Rashid-al-Din Fazl-Allah, “Oghuz-nameh”, which is part of his
2 CONC  extensive historical work Jami' al-tawarikh (Collection of Chronicles), the name Bayat means “rich, full of grace”.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Notable figures from this clan include: {...} Dede Korkut, legendary figure of the Oghuz  Fath-Ali Shah Qajar, the second Shah (king) of Qajar Iran.[12] Javad Khan, member of the Qajar dynasty, and the last khan of the Ganja Khana
2 CONC te from 1786 to 1804. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bayat_(tribe)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Bayāt tribes of Iran. The Bayāt presence in Iran is not attested before the 10th/16th century, when two separate groups are mentioned. The “genuine” or Āq (White) Bayāt tribe lived mainly around Kazzāz (34° N 49° 26’ E., south
2 CONC east of Hamadān) and then had 10,000 tents (Defense Mapping Agency, Names Gazetteer, p. 6; idem, 1984, s.v. “Bayāt”). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Although pre-16th-century evidence is lacking, it seems probable that these Bayāts came from northern Syria with the Āq Qoyunlū. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In Shah Ṭahmāsb’s reign begs from the tribe were governors of Hamadān and districts on the Ottoman frontier, and in Shah ʿAbbās I’s reign Bayāt amirs and troops were sent to Azerbaijan, with the results that Bayāts settled in that
2 CONC  province also. At the same time small groups established themselves at Darband and Šābrān. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Āq Bayāt tribe produced a large number of prominent amirs, whose names are mentioned in the chronicles of Ḥasan Rūmlū and Eskandar Beg and elsewhere. Although the Bayāts are reckoned in the Taḏkerat al-molūk ... ( https://www.
2 CONC iranicaonline.org/articles/bayat-an-important-turkish-tribe) to be less powerful than the Šāmlū and the Ḏu’l-Qadr, they are put fifth by Don Juan of Persia (1604), who himself belonged to this tribe, among the thirty-two noble fam
2 CONC ilies and clans “in whose hands the government is placed,” being “most noble and comparable to dukes.”  Many Bayāt amirs were appointed to high positions outside the tribal area, e.g., Gadā-ʿAlī Solṭān to the governorship of Mākū 
2 CONC in Shah ʿAbbās I’s reign, others to similar posts in the Īravān (Erevan) district, and Ḥosayn-ʿAlī Beg to the leadership of an embassy to Spain in 1006/1598 (Sümer, 1950, p. 385). 
2 CONT
2 CONT The second main group, distinguished by the name Qara (Black) Bayāt, lived in Khorasan, for the most part around Nīšāpūr. Moḥammad-Kāẓem, calls them the Nīšāpūrī Bayāts. They were much involved with the Turkman tribes of Ḵᵛārazm. 
2 CONC When Shah Esmāʿīl I conquered Khorasan, they acknowledged Safavid sovereignty. The Uzbek ruler ʿAbd-Allāh Khan (991-1006/1583-98), who seized much of Khorasan, placed them under the authority of his son ʿAbd-al-Moṇʿem Khan (ibid, 
2 CONC p. 387). Nevertheless they remained loyal to the Safavids and rendered such valuable services to Shah ʿAbbās I that he rewarded them with a promise of tax exemption. Qara Bayāt amirs continued to hold the governorship of Nīšāpūr i
2 CONC n Nāder Shah’s reign and thereafter. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition, there was a third Bayāt group, known as the Bayāt-e Šām or Bayāt-e Qājār, evidently, a branch of the Šām Bayātī of Syria and Anatolia. They must have come to Iran with the Qajar tribe in the wake of the Āq Qoyunlū. In
2 CONC  the 10th/16th century the Qajars lived in Azerbaijan, mainly around Ganja and Barḏaʿa; it is not clear which of the Qajar amirs were of Bayāt descent. In the 12th/18th century a village near Ganja bore the name of the Bayāt-e Šām
2 CONC  and a certain Moḥammad-ʿAlī Khan, who belonged to this tribe, was an amir in the service of ʿĀdel Shah Afšār. Under the Qajars, particularly in Fatḥ-ʿAlī Shah’s reign (1212-50/1797-1834), several amirs from the tribe rose to prom
2 CONC inence.
