1 OCCU EMPRESS CONSORT AND GRANDNIECE OF EMPEROR JOHN III DOUKAS VATATZES OF NICAEA
1 TITL Princess
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodora_Doukaina_Vatatzina
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodora Doukaina Vatatzaina (Greek: Θεοδώρα Δούκαινα Βατάτζαινα) (c. 1240 – 4 March 1303) was the Empress consort of Michael VIII Palaiologos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodora was a daughter of Ioannes Doukas Vatatzes (born 1215) and wife Eudokia Angelina. The names of her parents were recorded by George Acropolites.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her paternal grandparents were sebastokrator Isaakios Doukas Vatatzes (died 1261) and his unnamed wife. Isaakios was an older brother of John III Doukas Vatatzes who reigned in the Empire of Nicaea from 1221 to 1254.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her maternal grandparents were protostrator John Komnenos Angelos and his unnamed wife. His name indicates him being a member of the Angelos family. However his exact relations to the reigning members of the family are not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT John III and Isaac were probably sons of domestikos Basil Vatatzes, killed in battle against forces of Ivan Asen I of Bulgaria in 1193. Their mother was an unnamed cousin of the Emperors Isaac II Angelos and Alexios III Angelos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Europäische Stammtafeln: Stammtafeln zur Geschichte der Europäischen Staaten (1978) by Detlev Schwennicke suggests the wife of Basil to be a daughter of Isaac Angelos, strategos of Cilicia in 1170.
2 CONT
2 CONT The elder Isaac is given by the same book as a son of Konstantinos Angelos, Admiral of Sicily (c. 1085 – aft. July 1166) and his wife Theodora Komnene. Theodora was the youngest daughter of Emperor Alexios I Komnenos and Irene Dou
2 CONC kaina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Acropolites mentions that the father of Theodora died in young manhood. Her mother died in the early 1250s. Leaving Theodora to be raised by her great-uncle John III who was said to have "loved her like a daughter".
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1253, John III arranged the marriage of Theodora to Michael Palaiologos. Her husband was a great-grandson of Alexios III Angelos and Euphrosyne Doukaina Kamaterina. He was steadily rising in distinction due to a combination of 
2 CONC familial connections and personal abilities.
2 CONT
2 CONT John III died on 3 November 1254. He was succeeded by his only son Theodore II Laskaris. Theodore was a first cousin to John Doukas Vatatzes,uncle of Theodora. Thus Theodora and Michael remained closely related to the new emperor.
2 CONC  However Theodore's administration relied on bureaucrats from the middle classes instead of members of the great aristocratic families.
2 CONT
2 CONT This caused considerable opposition by the nobility to the emperor and his chief minister, megas domestikos George Mouzalon. Michael became one of the leaders of the aristocratic faction. In 1256, Michael was accused of consprinin
2 CONC g with representatives of Kaykaus II, Sultan of Rûm. He went into exile or self-exile to the court of Kaykaus in Iconium. Whether Theodora followed her husband to Iconium or remained with her cousin in Nicaea is uncertain.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1258, Theodore recalled Michael from exile, giving assurances for his safety and position. Michael was restored to his former position of megas kontostaulos, chief of the Frankish mercenaries. Theodore suffered from epilepsy an
2 CONC d died during a seizure on 18 August 1258.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodore was succeeded by his only son John IV Laskaris, second cousin of Theodora. His regent and guardian was originally George Mouzalon. Arsenius Autoreianus, Ecumenical Patriarch of Constantinople in exile was associate guardi
2 CONC an. The regency of Mouzalon proved short-lived and ended with his assassination. Michael replaced him as regent and guardian. Arsenius remained associate guardian in name but took refuge in the monastery of Paschasius and had no c
2 CONC ontact with his charge. Theodora was married to the de facto ruler of the Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Empress
2 CONT
2 CONT As regent, Michael was self-appointed Megas Doux in late Summer, 1258 and Despotes in November. On 1 January 1259, Michael was proclaimed co-emperor at Nymphaion. He was still supported by members of his old aristocratic faction a
2 CONC nd also by a new ally, the Republic of Genoa. Theodora was the only empress at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 25 July 1261, Alexios Strategopoulos captured Constantinople, capital of the Latin Empire. Most troops of the rival Empire were in campaign and would never return to their capital. Latin Emperor Baldwin II of Courtenay escaped 
2 CONC in a Venetian galley to Negropont and would remain in exile for the rest of his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael took advantage of the success of his general. He entered Constantinople on 15 August, 1261 and was soon followed by Theodora and their children. In September, Michael was crowned Emperor, Theodora an Augusta and their elde
2 CONC st surviving son Andronikos a co-emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT John IV was left behind in Nicaea. He was deposed and blinded on 25 December 1261, his eleventh birthday. He spent the rest of his life as a monk and died c. 1305.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodora remained Empress consort until the death of her husband on 11 December 1282. She survived him by almost twenty-one years. His mistress Diplovatatzina was probably related to Theodora but Pachymeres does not state the exac
2 CONC t relation of the two women. "Diplo" is Greek for "double".
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodora and Michael VIII had seven children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Manuel Palaiologos (c. 1254–1259)
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronikos II Palaiologos (1259–1332)
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine Palaiologos (1261–1306)
2 CONT
2 CONT Eirene Palaiologina, who married emperor Ivan Asen III of Bulgaria
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Palaiologina, who married Demetrios Angelos. Demetrios was a son of Michael II Komnenos Doukas and Theodora Petraliphaina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eudokia Palaiologina, who married Emperor John II of Trebizond
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodora Palaiologina, who married King David VI Narin of Georgia and Imereti
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodora Doukaina Vatatzaina
2 CONT
