1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Yuri Narimuntovich (born around 1326 – died after 1398) – Prince of Belz (1348–1377, 1383–1387) and Kholmsk (1366–1370), the youngest son of Prince Narimunt of Pinsk (d. 1348) and the grandson of the Grand Duke of 
2 CONC Lithuania Gediminas.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1348, Yuri Narimuntovich received the northern lands of the Galicia-Volyn principality (Belz and Rozhyn castles), occupied by the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, as an appanage. As the Prince of Belz, he recognized the suzerainty of 
2 CONC the Grand Duke of Lithuania.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1352, Prince Yuri Narimuntovich successfully defended the city of Belz in Volyn from the Polish-Hungarian troops under the command of the King of Hungary, Louis (Lajos) the Great. Still, he was forced to recognize formal depend
2 CONC ence on the latter. Under the terms of the truce, Yuri Narimuntovich received the city of Kremenets, which was under the joint control of Poland and the Grand Duchy of Lithuania.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1366, as the Prince of Belz and Kholm, he recognized himself as a vassal of the King of Poland, Casimir III the Great, who transferred to him the Kholm principality, which the Poles had occupied.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1370, after the death of the King of Poland, Casimir the Great, and the accession of Louis the Great to the Polish throne, Prince Yuri Narimuntovich broke off vassal relations with Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1376, he took part in the devastating campaign of the Lithuanian princes Kestutis and Lubart against Lesser Poland, reaching Sandomierz and Tarnow.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1377, Louis (Lajos) the Great's punitive expedition resulted in Yuri Narimuntovich losing his possessions, retaining only Lubaczów. Yuri Narimuntovich's fortresses of Grabowiec, Chelm, Belz, Gorodlo, and Vsevolozh were annexed 
2 CONC to the possessions of the Hungarian king.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the "Livonian Chronicle" by Hermann Wartberg, this event is described as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT "In the same year, at the same time, the Hungarian king was with a large army in the lands of the infidels, namely Lademar. Having devastated part of them, the same king besieged the castle of Belze, where George, son of Narmante,
2 CONC  resided. However, when the king stood near the castle for almost seven weeks, George began to fear danger for himself and his people and yielded the castle to the king along with the land and people. The king accepted the castle 
2 CONC and gave it to his Polish advisers. And so this castle, where the heretics lived, now belongs to the Hungarian crown. The king then took George with his wife and sons and gave him in exchange a castle in Hungary with people, land,
2 CONC  and all the possessions and property belonging to it. During the siege of the above-mentioned castle, the king sent a detachment that captured two other Russian castles."
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1378-1379 he was in Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1379, the Grand Duke of Lithuania Jagiello Olgerdovich sent Yuri Narimuntovich to Veliky Novgorod, where he assumed power on behalf of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania. Although the Novgorodians received the prince well, they remai
2 CONC ned loyal to the Grand Duke of Moscow, Dmitry Donskoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1386, Prince Yuri Narimuntovich participated in the coronation ceremony of the Grand Duke of Lithuania Vladislav Jagiello in Krakow. In 1388, he went over to the side of the Teutonic Knights-Crusaders. From 1392, Yuri Narimunto
2 CONC vich was in the service of the Grand Duke of Lithuania Vitovt.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is possible that Yuri "the Nose" Vasilyevich Narimuntovich, Prince of Pinsk, participated on behalf of the Grand Duke of Lithuania Vytautas in the 1398 signing of the Salines Treaty with the Teutonic Order.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
