2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod III Yuryevich, or Vsevolod the Big Nest (Russian: Все́волод III Ю́рьевич Большо́е Гнездо́) (1154 – 1212), was the Grand Prince of Vladimir during whose long reign (1177–1212) the city reached the zenith of its glory.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod's Christian name was Dmitry, so he dedicated his palace church to St. Demetrius, his patron saint.Contents 1 Family 2 Reign 3 Marriage and children 4 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Vsevolod was the tenth or eleventh son of Yury Dolgoruky, who founded the town Dmitrov to commemorate the site of his birth. Nikolai Mikhailovich Karamzin was the first to speculate that Vsevolod's mother Helene was a Greek
2 CONC  princess, for after her husband's death she took Vsevolod with her to Constantinople.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was at the chivalric court of the Komnenoi that he spent his youth. On his return from the Byzantine Empire to Rus in 1170, Vsevolod supposedly visited Tbilisi, as a local chronicle records that that year the Georgian king ente
2 CONC rtained his nephew from Constantinople and married him to his relative, an Ossetian princess.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign In 1173, Vsevolod was briefly installed on the Kievan throne and taken prisoner by two Smolensk princes who captured the town. Ransomed a year later, he took his brother Mikhalko's side in his struggle against the powerful b
2 CONC oyars of Rostov and Suzdal. Upon Mikhalko's death, Vsevolod succeeded him in Vladimir. He promptly subjugated the boyars and systematically raided the Volga peoples, notably Volga Bulgaria. He installed his puppets on the throne o
2 CONC f Novgorod and married his daughters to princes of Chernigov and Kiev. Even the sovereigns of far-away Halych had to acknowledge his suzerainty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod showed little mercy to those who disobeyed his word. In 1180 and 1187, he punished the princes of Ryazan by ousting them from their lands. In 1207, he burnt to the ground both Ryazan and Belgorod. His military fame spread
2 CONC  quickly. The Tale of Igor's Campaign, thought to be written during Vsevolod's reign, addresses him thus: Great prince Vsevolod! Don't you think of flying here from afar to safeguard the paternal golden throne of Kiev? For you can
2 CONC  with your oars scatter in drops the Volga, and with your helmets scoop dry the Don.
2 CONT
2 CONT But Kievan matters concerned Vsevolod little in the latter part of his reign. He concentrated on making his own capital, Vladimir, the most glorious city of Rus. His Ossetian wife, Maria Shvarnovna, who devoted herself to the work
2 CONC s of piety and founded several convents, was glorified by the Russian church as a saint. By her Vsevolod had no less than twelve children, thus earning for himself the sobriquet Big Nest. Four of them — Konstantin, George, Yarosla
2 CONC v and Sviatoslav — succeeded him as Grand Dukes of Vladimir. He died on April 12, 1212 and was buried at the Assumption Cathedral in Vladimir.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children Vsevolod married first Maria Shvarnovna. Her origins are disputed. She has been variously identified as Ossetian, Alan and Moravian. They had at least fourteen children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sbislava Vsevolodovna (born 26 October 1178). Vseslava Vsevolodovna. Married Rostislav Yaroslavich, Prince of Snov. He was a son of Yaroslav Vsevolodovich, Prince of Chernigov. His paternal grandfather was Vsevolod II of Kiev. Ver
2 CONC choslava Vsevolodovna. Married Rostislav II of Kiev. Konstantin of Rostov (18 May 1186 - 2 February 1218). Boris Vsevolodovich. (c. 1187 - 1238). Gleb Vsevolodovich (d. 29 September 1189). Yuri II of Vladimir (1189 - 4 March 1238)
2 CONC . Yaroslav II of Vladimir (8 February 1191 - 30 September 1246). Helena Vsevolodovna (d. 1204). Vladimir Vsevolodovich, Prince of Yuryev-Polsky (25 October 1194 - 6 January 1229). Sviatoslav III of Vladimir (27 March 1196 - 3 Febr
2 CONC uary 1252). Ivan Vsevodovich, Prince of Starodub (28 November 1197 - after 1247). Pelagea Vsevolodovna. Anna Vsevolodovna. Married Vladimir, Prince of Belgorod (d. 1239). Maria died in 1205 or 1206. Vsevolod married Liubov Vasilko
2 CONC vna in 1209. She was a daughter of Vasilko Bryacheslavich, Prince of Vitebsk. They had no known children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by Mikhalko Yuriyevich Grand Prince of Vladimir-Suzdal 1176–1212 Succeeded by Yuri II
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by Roman I Grand Prince of Kiev Succeeded by Rurik
2 CONT
