1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Duke Yuri Patrikeyevich (born ca. 1384 - d. after 1439) - boyar in Moscow and Moscovian Voivode, during the reign of the Grand Dukes Vasily I Dmitrievich and Vasily II Vasilyevich the Dark, the second son of Duke P
2 CONC atrikey Narimuntovich and Elena. He came from the Gedimiides dynasty and was the ancestor of the Patrikeyev Dukes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yuri Patrikeyevich had brothers: Fyodor Patrikeyevich Khovansky and Alexander Patrikeyevich Koretsky.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Duke Yury was married to the daughter of the Grand Duke Vasili(?); from him came the Bulgakovs, Shchenyatevs, Kurakins, Golitsyns.''
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Yuri's father, Duke Patrikei Narimuntovich, and Duke Švitrigaila left the Grand Duchy of Lithuania in 1408 to serve the Grand Duke of Moscow Vasily I. Due to his close relationship with the Grand Dukes of Lithuania, Patrikei was r
2 CONC eceived with honor. His son Yuri enjoyed the respect of Vasily I and, in 1418, became related to the Grand Duke, thanks to which he took first place among the Moscow boyars. According to sources, '''Yuri Patrikeyevich''' "ran" man
2 CONC y of Vasily I's boyars.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1425, Yuri Patrikeyevich was mentioned as one of the witnesses in Grand Duke Vasily I's will.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1433, Grand Duke Vasily II sent Yuri Patrikeyevich as the head of an army to Kostroma to fight the Galician princes Vasily Kosoy and Dmitry Shemyaka. However, in the battle near the Kus River, Yuri's army was routed, and he him
2 CONC self was captured.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1434, he was mentioned as a Voivode in Kostroma.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1437, Yuri Patrikeyevich, by order of the Grand Duke, went as an ambassador to Novgorod to ask the city's residents for a "black forest," but they refused him.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1439, Vasily II, who went to gather troops, fearing a raid by Ulu-Muhammad, left Yuri Patrikeyevich as the first commander for the defense of Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT After 1439, there is no news of Yuri Patrikeyevich. His younger son Ivan inherited his place in the boyar duma, while his eldest son, Vasily, apparently died early.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriage and Children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the hypothesis of the 19th-century historian A. V. Ekzemplyarsky, Yuri Patrikeyevich's wife was Maria Vasilievna, the daughter of the Grand Duke of Moscow Vasily I Dmitrievich. But in the 21st century, historian V. A.
2 CONC  Kuchkin questioned the version of Maria's existence. The researcher noted that the text of the Voskresenskaya Chronicle, to which Ekzemplyarsky referred, only lists the children of Yuri Patrikeyevich, named as the son-in-law of t
2 CONC he Grand Duke, while the name of his wife is absent. Kuchkin points out that neither the chronicles, or acts, or early genealogical records confirm that Grand Duke Vasily I Dmitrievich had a daughter named Maria. In addition, seve
2 CONC ral sources name Duchess Anna as the wife of Yuri Patrikeyevich, who could not have been the daughter of Vasily I: he did have a daughter, Anna Vasilyevna, but she was married to the Byzantine Emperor John VIII Palaeologus and die
2 CONC d in Constantinople in August 1417 from the plague. According to Kuchkin, '''the boyar's wife was Anna Dmitrievna, the sister of Vasily I'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * son Vasily (d. January 1450). His descendants were the Dukes Bulgakov, Golitsyn, Kurakin, and Shchenyatev.
2 CONT * son Ivan Gvozd (1419-1499), boyar 1461/1462, voivode, in 1499, he became a monk.
2 CONT * daughter Elena; husband: Ivan Mikhailovich Chelyadnin, boyar. 
