1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Based on the definitive archival research from the historians of Maragheh (including the works of Dr. Asghar Mohammadzadeh), Hasan Ali Bey Moghadam was the son of Aqa Khan II (Aqa Khan-e Sani). 
2 CONT The Direct Lineage & Context
2 CONT According to primary regional records, when the first dynastic seat established by the original patriarch ended, the tribal succession flowed precisely as follows:
2 CONT 1.	Agha Khan I (Ghazi Soltan): The founding patriarch who migrated the tribe to Maragheh in 1609.
2 CONT 2.	Aqa Khan II (Aqa Khan-e Sani): His eldest son, who ruled from 1625 to 1648 alongside his brother Niyaz.
2 CONT 3.	Hasan Ali Bey Moghadam: The son of Aqa Khan II. 
2 CONT His Role under Nader Shah 
2 CONT When Hasan Ali Bey stepped up as the representative and Vakil (advocate/attorney) for the Moghadam clan during the chaotic transition to Afsharid rule, he bridged the gap left by the 17th-century imperial disruptions. 
2 CONT The contemporary historical chronicle Tarikh-e Alam-aray-e Naderi records a famous anecdote about Hasan Ali Bey's leadership. When Nader Shah approached the city to liberate it from the 7-year Ottoman occupation, Hasan Ali Bey gat
2 CONC hered 17 of the most respected grey-bearded elders (Mahasin-e Sefidan) of the Moghadam tribe. They met Nader Shah's royal caravan two miles outside the town and actively rolled in the dust and dirt ahead of the Shah's horses all t
2 CONC he way into the city center as an extreme display of absolute loyalty. Nader Shah implored them to stop, but they declared they had sworn a holy oath to throw their bodies before the Safavid/Qizilbash liberators. 
2 CONT Impressed by this display, Nader Shah rewarded Hasan Ali Bey's faction by solidifying their administrative rights over the city.
