2 CONT Helena of Znojmo (Polish: Helena znojemska b. 1145 – d. 1202 or 1206[1]) was a Bohemian princess member of the Premyslid dynasty. She was Duchess of Moravia (1165–1194), hence the name Znojmo after town, and by marriage Duchess of
2 CONC  Masovia (1186–1194) and Duchess-regent of Krakow, Sandomierz and Masovia on behalf of her minor son during 1194–1199/1200.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the daughter of Duke Conrad II of Znojmo and his wife Maria of Serbia, daughter of Uroš I of Rascia. Helena was probably named after her maternal aunt, Queen Helena of Hungary, wife of King Béla II.[2] [edit] Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Helen married the Casimir II the Just, youngest son of Bolesław III Wrymouth, probably shortly after his return from captivity[3].
2 CONT
2 CONT When on 11 May 1194 Helen's husband died, presumably as a result of heart attack,[4] he left her with their two minor sons on whose behalf she took regency of Małopolska and Masovia with blessings from Bishop of Kraków Fulko and K
2 CONC rakow Voivode.
2 CONT
2 CONT Helen's regency was not an easy one, because other princes threatened the minors., their own uncle Mieszko III the Old was after Kraków. Bloody stocks, which were the culminating moment of the Battle of Mozgawa between Helen and M
2 CONC ieszko, it lasted until 1198 when they reached an agreement with Duke of Wielkopolska. Mieszko would take Krakow, in return for which he gave Helen and her sons Kuyavia. Independent authority over the inheritance was taken on by L
2 CONC eszek the White who took over in 1199 or 1200. The great political and intelligent Helen testied words written in the chronicle of Kadlubek Vincent, who certainly knew the princess in person. According to historians Helen was "a w
2 CONC ise woman than a woman's wisdom"(misinterpret translation?).
2 CONT
2 CONT Helen of Znojmo died between 1202 and 1206 [5], probably on April 2[6].
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Children===
2 CONT
2 CONT Helen and Casimir had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT *   1. Maria (1164–1194), married in November 1178 to Prince Vsevolod IV of Kiev.
2 CONT *    2. Casimir (ca. 1165? – 1 March 1167).
2 CONT *    3. Bolesław (ca. 1168/71 – 16 April 1182). He died accidentally, after falling from a tree.
2 CONT *    4. Odon, died in infancy. Only named in the Polish Wikipedia Biography of Casimir II the Just
2 CONT *    5. Adelaide (ca. 1177/84 – 8 December 1211), foundress of the convent of St. Jakob in Sandomierz. Only named in the Polish Wikipedia Biography of Casimir II the Just
2 CONT *    6. Leszek the White (ca. 1186/87 – Marcinkow, 23 November 1227).
2 CONT *    7. Konrad (ca. 1187/88 – 31 August 1247).[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] References
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. ^ Life Dates for: K. Jasiński, Rodowód pierwszych Piastów, Poznań 2004, p. 266–268
2 CONT    2. ^ According to recently historiography; see K. Jasiński, op.cit., p. 266. Until the 1970s prevailed the erroneous theory who give a Rurikid origin to Helena as daughter of Rostislav Mstislavich, Grand Prince of Kiev, followi
2 CONC ng the chronicles of Jan Długosz, who deduced her parentage on the basis of her Russian-sounding name.
2 CONT    3. ^ K. Jasiński cit., pp.266–267
2 CONT    4. ^ R. Horn, Polish królobójcy, Warsaw 1994, p. 27
2 CONT    5. ^ MORAVIA, Medieval Lands
2 CONT    6. ^ According to John Dlugosz Helena still alive December 24, 1211 However, this message has already been challenged by O. Balzera. Cf. O. Balzer, Genealogia Piastów, 2nd. ed., Kraków 2005, p. 327
2 CONT    7. ^ There is however a very unlikely possibility, but which can not be completely ruled out that Helen was the second wife of Casimir the Just. But no mention about this in the sources, hence this hypothesis is merely a logica
2 CONC l structure, which even more reduces its credibility. Cf. K. Jasinski cit., P. 267
2 CONT
