1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ivan II Ivanovich the Fair (Иван II Иванович Красный in Russian), Grand Prince of Moscow
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: 30 March 1326
2 CONT
2 CONT Died: 13 November 1359
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan I 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouses: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fedosia of Bryansk
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexandra Velyaminova
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dmitry Ivanovich Donskoi
2 CONT
2 CONT Liuba Ivanovna
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan Ivanovich of Zvenigorod
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Ivanovna
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor	: Simeon http://www.geni.com/people/Simeon-the-Proud-Grand-Prince-of-Moscow/361886414530007915#/tab/overview
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor: Dmitry I http://www.geni.com/people/Saint-Dmitry-I-of-the-Don-Grand-Prince-of-Moscow/6000000000671874700
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivan_II_of_Moscow
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan II was the second son of Ivan Kalita who succeeded his brother Simeon the Proud, who died of the Black Death, as Grand Prince of Moscow and Grand Prince of Vladimir in 1353. Until that date, he had ruled the towns of Ruza and
2 CONC  Zvenigorod.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon succeeding his brother and because of increased civil strife among the Golden Horde, Ivan briefly toyed with the idea of abandoning traditional Moscow allegiance to the Mongols and allying himself with Lithuania, a growing po
2 CONC wer in the west. This policy was quickly abandoned and Ivan asserted his allegiance to the Golden Horde.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contemporaries described Ivan as a pacific, apathetic ruler, who didn't flinch even when Algirdas of Lithuania captured his father-in-law's capital, Bryansk. He also allowed Oleg of Riazan to burn villages on his territory. Howeve
2 CONC r, Orthodox churchmen aided consolidating the power of the Grand Prince. He received much aid from the capable Metropolitan Alexius. Like his brother, Ivan II was not as successful as his father or grandfather with regards to terr
2 CONC itorial expansion. Nevertheless, he was able to annex areas southwest of Moscow, including the areas of Borovsk, and Vereya.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is buried in the Cathedral of the Archangel Michael in Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan was married twice. In 1341, Ivan married his first wife Fedosia Dmitrievna of Bryansk. She was a daughter of Dmitry Romanovich, Prince of Bryansk. She died childless in Autumn, 1342.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan remained a widower for three years. In 1345, Ivan married his second wife Alexandra Ivanovna Velyaminova. She was a daughter of Vasily Velyaminov, a mayor of Moscow. They had at least four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Dmitri Donskoi (12 October 1350 – 19 May 1389). His successor in the Grand Duchy of Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Liuba Ivanovna. Assumed the name "Anna" following her marriage to Dmitry Mikhailovich, Prince of Volhynia (d. 1399). Her husband was a son of Karijotas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan Ivanovich, Prince of Zvenigorod (c. 1356 - October, 1364).
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Ivanovna.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ivan II of Moscow
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan II Ivanovich the Fair (Иван II Иванович Красный in Russian) (March 30, 1326 – November 13, 1359) was the second son of Ivan Kalita who succeeded his brother Simeon the Proud, who died of the Black Death, as Grand Prince of Mo
2 CONC scow and Grand Prince of Vladimir in 1353. Until that date, he had ruled the towns of Ruza and Zvenigorod.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon succeeding his brother and because of increased civil strife among the Golden Horde, Ivan briefly toyed with the idea of abandoning traditional Moscow allegiance to the Mongols and allying himself with Lithuania, a growing po
2 CONC wer in the west. This policy was quickly abandoned and Ivan asserted his allegiance to the Golden Horde.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Contemporaries described Ivan as a pacific, apathetic ruler, who didn't flinch even when Algirdas of Lithuania captured his father-in-law's capital, Bryansk.[2] He also allowed Oleg of Riazan to burn villages on his territory. How
2 CONC ever, Orthodox churchmen aided consolidating the power of the Grand Prince. He received much aid from the capable Metropolitan Alexius. Like his brother, Ivan II was not as successful as his father or grandfather with regards to t
2 CONC erritorial expansion. Nevertheless, he was able to annex areas southwest of Moscow, including the areas of Borovsk, and Vereya.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is buried in the Cathedral of the Archangel Michael in Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriages and children
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan was married twice. In 1341, Ivan married his first wife Fedosia Dmitrievna of Bryansk. She was a daughter of Dmitry Romanovich, Prince of Bryansk. She died childless in Autumn, 1342.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan remained a widower for three years. In 1345, Ivan married his second wife Alexandra Ivanovna Velyaminova. She was a daughter of Vasily Velyaminov, a mayor of Moscow. They had at least four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Dmitri Donskoi (12 October 1350 - 19 May 1389). His successor in the Grand Duchy of Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Liuba Ivanovna. Assumed the name "Anna" following her marriage to Dmitry Mikhailovich, Prince of Volhynia (d. 1399). Her husband was a son of Karijotas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan Ivanovich, Prince of Zvenigorod (c. 1356 - October, 1364).
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Ivanovna.
