1 OCCU 3d wife of Giedymin / Княгиня Литовская
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} There are three different versions of who Giedymin's - Gediminas' father, grandfather and great-grandfather are: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Polish-Lithuanian version: Pucuverus Rex Lethowie, Trojden and Wołk. 
2 CONT 2. Samogitian version: Butvydas - Pucuwerus, Dausprungas - Dowsprunk and Ringaudas - Ryngold. 
2 CONT 3. Russian version: Витень - Witeń, Пройденъ - Projden and Видъ - Wid. 
2 CONT
2 CONT And these three versions are contradictory and irreconcilable, and this controversy will continue forever. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------- 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jewna 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Jewna''' (Belarusian: Еўна, Lithuanian: Jaunė, literally, young woman; died ca. 1344) was daughter of Prince Ivan of Polotsk, she was wife of Gediminas, the Grand Duke of Lithuania, Ruthenia and Samogitia (1316–1341). She is me
2 CONC ntioned in written sources only once – the Bychowiec Chronicle, a late and unreliable source. Therefore some historians cast a serious doubt on her existence, but modern reference works still widely cite her as the ancestress of t
2 CONC he Gediminids dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are considerable doubts about how many wives Gediminas had. The Bychowiec Chronicle mentions three wives: Vida from Courland, Olga from Smolensk, and Jewna. Some modern historians suggest that Gediminas had two wives, one fr
2 CONC om local pagan nobles, and Jewna, an Orthodox. S. C. Rowell claims that Gediminas had only one wife, an unknown pagan duchess. He argues that an important marriage to a Ruthenian or Polish princess like Jewna would have been noted
2 CONC  in contemporary sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Bychowiec Chronicle mentions that after Jewna's death, brothers Algirdas and Kęstutis became displeased with Jaunutis, whom Gediminas chose as his heir. Soon they deposed Jaunutis. This episode is interpreted that weak Jaunuti
2 CONC s was protected by his mother. If such interpretation was accurate, then it would testify the power and influence of queen mother in pagan Lithuania
