2 DATE 1343
1 OCCU Prince of Polock 1315 –1342, Polocko kun, not listed in Vatican Gediminids genealogy
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Wojn''', Wojn-Basil - (? - before 1338) - Prince of Polotsk.
2 CONT
2 CONT The origin and circumstances of Wojn are unknown. He was first mentioned in 1326, when together with the Minsk prince Fyodor Svyatoslavovich at the head of the embassy Gediminas went to Novgorod to conclude a peace treaty with the
2 CONC  Novgorod land and the Livonian Order. Together with Fedor Sviatoslavovich, he was called "brotherly" of Gediminas, which testifies to his vassal dependence on the GDL while maintaining a high status, because he was called "brothe
2 CONC r", apparently the youngest, Gediminas, not his son. No more mention in the sources. It is possible that he died during the Livonian Crusaders' expeditions to Polotsk in 1333 - 1334, because in 1338 Narymont Gediminovich was menti
2 CONC oned as a Polotsk prince.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a son Lubka.
2 CONT
2 CONT Warrior (before 1295 - 1333/1334) - Prince of Polotsk (1320 - 1333/1334) [1].
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian name 
2 CONT
2 CONT In early Belarusian historiography, the Warrior was identified with Prince Vasily of Minsk, so there is evidence that the Christian name of the Warrior was Vasily [1], later such an opinion was found to be incorrect.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to J. Wolf, the third son of Budivid, the brother of Viten and Gediminas [1] [2]. Circumstances and time of occupation of the Polotsk chair by the Warriors are unknown. For the first time, and already as the prince of Po
2 CONC lotsk, it is mentioned in 1326 [3], when together with the Minsk prince. Basil and Rzhev book. Fedor Svyatoslavich, went to Novgorod at the head of the Gediminas Embassy to conclude a peace treaty with the Novgorod land and the Li
2 CONC vonian Order. No more sources are mentioned. He probably died during the campaigns of the Livonian knights in Polotsk (1333, 1334) [3], because in 1338 Narymont Gediminovich was called the Polotsk prince.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a son, Lubka, who in 1342 commanded a detachment in Algerd's campaign to help Pskov [1] and died in battle with the Livonian knights.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the reign of the Warrior there was a further rapprochement of Polotsk with other Belarusian lands (Minsk, Novgorod, Turau and Pinsk). This was facilitated by the struggle against the Crusaders, who often attacked Polotsk (1
2 CONC 325, 1333, 1334).
2 CONT
