2 CONT Rostislav I of Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Rostislav Mstislavich (Russian: Ростислав Мстиславич) (c. 1110 – 1167), Kniaz' (Prince) of Smolensk (1125–1160), Novgorod (1154) and Velikiy Kniaz (Grand Prince) of Kiev (1154, 1159–1167). He was the son of Mstislav I of Kiev and 
2 CONC Christina Ingesdotter of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Yaroslav II of Kiev was driven out of Novgorod, Rostislav was invited to become the ruler of Novgorod. He accepted, and became the prince on April 17, 1154. Then, learning that Iziaslav II had died, Rostislav left Novgorod t
2 CONC o take the Kievan throne. Indignant that their prince had abandoned them and angered that "he did not make order among them, but tore them more apart", the citizens of Novgorod drove out Rostislav's son, David, who was their gover
2 CONC nor. They replaced him with Mstislav Yurievich, the son of Yury Dolgoruky.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rostislav ruled Kiev for one week before Iziaslav III of Kiev forced him to flee to Chernigov.
2 CONT
