2 CONC ed with the Swabian noblewoman Salomea, sister of the Duchesses of Bohemia and Olomouc.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage negotiations, led by Bishop Otto of Bamberg, ended successfully, and between March and July of 1115 took place the wedding of Bolesław III Wrymouth and Salomea of Berg. By the end of that year, the new Duchess gave bi
2 CONC rth a son named Leszek, the first of the thirteen children who she bore to her husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT Salomea began to participate actively in the Polish politics on behalf of her children; she feared that her stepson Władysław (Bolesław III's oldest son from his first marriage) would succeeded his father as sole ruler and her son
2 CONC s would be at the mercy of his half-brother. In 1125 the powerful Piotr Włostowic was forced to resign his post of Count Palatine; the main instigator of this decision was probably Salomea, who replaced him with Wszebor, a man she
2 CONC  considered more likely to support her and her sons against Władysław.
