0 @N6000000002188099041@ NOTE Barlas
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Barlas or Birlas, a Mongolian nomadic conferdeation in Central Asia, was the chief tribe of the Timurid emperors who ruled much of Central Asia, Iran, and Hindustan. The word Barlas means to have a heart of steel. [citation needed
1 CONC ]
1 CONT
1 CONT In the famous book 'Mongolian Secret Brief' written during the times of Gengghis Khan & Ogedey Khan records that the Barlas Clan was derived from Bodonchir who is the direct ancestor of the Gengghis Khan's Borjigin Clan. It says a
1 CONC s follows: "Barim Shibatu Habich was born from Bodonchir... Habich Baatar is father of Menen Tudun. Menen Tudun had seven sons:Hachi Hulug, Hachin, Hachiu, Hachula, Haraldai, Hachiun, Nachin Baatar... Hachi Hulug is father of Haid
1 CONC u (Haidu is 6-level grandfather of Gengghis Khan)... Hachiu is father of Baruladai... Baruladai is the ancestor of Barlas Clan...".
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External link
1 CONT Timur Empire 
1 CONT This article related to Central Asian history is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it.
0 @N6000000002188002149@ NOTE Yesugei
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Yesugei Baghatur or Yesugei The Brave (11XX–1180) (Yesügei, Yesükhei) was a Mongol Khan (or ruler). He was the son of Bartan-baghatur, who was the son of Qabul, who was recognized as a Khagan by the Jin Dynasty. Qabul Khagan was, 
1 CONC in turn, the grandson of the Mongol chief Qaidu, the first to try to unite all of the Mongols.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yesugei was chief of the Kiyad (possibly the Taichiut or Yakka) khanate between approximately 1153 and 1180. He had four sons and one daughter by his chief wife, Hoelun: Temujin (son, later known as Genghis Khan), Kasar (son), Kha
1 CONC ji'un (son), Temuga (son), and Temulin (daughter). He also had two sons by a lesser wife: Bektor and Beigutai. He was poisoned by Tartars while sharing a meal on the way home from finding his son Temujin (Genghis Khan) a wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yesugei's wife was Oyelun (Oelun, Ho’elun, Hoelun), a daughter of the Olkunut forest tribe. Oyelun was abducted by Yesugei and his brothers from her newlywed husband of the Merkit tribe as they were traveling back to the Merkit ca
1 CONC mp. Yesugei then made Oyelun his chief wife, a rare honor since Khans had several wives, and only one could bear his heirs.
0 @N6000000002188002161@ NOTE Hoelun
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Hoelun was the mother of Mongol Khan Genghis Khan and the grandmother of Ogedei Khan, first emperor of Yuan Dynasty. She was the wife of Yesükhei, the chief of the Kiyad clan. After Yesükhei's death, his clan abandoned Hoelun and 
1 CONC his sons including Genghis Khan (Temujin by birth). Therefore Genghis Khan was raised in the harsh environment of the Mongolian steppes. According to Mongolian legend, Hoelun taught Genghis and his brothers the basics of unity and
1 CONC  support for one another, even though Temujin killed his brother in a disagreement over hunting spoils.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hoelun was born to the Olkunut tribe. She later married a member of the Merkit tribe, but was abducted by Yesugei on her way back to the Merkit camp. Yesugei made Hoelun his chief wife. This was an honor, since only the chief wife
1 CONC  could give birth to his heirs.
0 @N6000000002188002111@ NOTE Genghis Khan
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Genghis Khan  
1 CONT Birth name: Temüjin Borjigin 
1 CONT Family name: Borjigin 
1 CONT Title: Khagan* of Mongol Empire
1 CONT
1 CONT Birth: c. 1162 
1 CONT Place of birth: Hentiy, Mongolia 
1 CONT Death: August 18, 1227 
1 CONT Dates of reign: 1206 –August 18, 1227 
1 CONT Succeeded by: Ögedei Khan 
1 CONT Marriage: Börte Ujin, Kulan, Yisugen,
1 CONT Yisui, many others 
1 CONT Children: Borjigin Jochi, son 
1 CONT Borjigin Chagatai, son 
1 CONT Borjigin Ögedei, son 
1 CONT Borjigin Tolui, son 
1 CONT Others 
1 CONT
1 CONT Genghis Khan (help·info), (c. 1162[1]–August 18, 1227) (IPA: [?i?g?s ?a??], Mongolian: ?????? ????, Chinese: ????, Turkic: Chengez Khan or Chinggis Khan, Chingis Khan, Jenghis Khan, Chinggis Qan, etc.), was a Mongol political and 
1 CONC military leader or Khan who united the Mongol tribes and founded the Mongol Empire (?? ?????? ???), (1206–1368), the largest contiguous empire in world history. Born Temüjin (Mongolian: ????????, Traditional Chinese: ???; pinyin: 
1 CONC Tiemùzhen), he forged a powerful army based on meritocracy, to become one of the most successful military leaders in history.
1 CONT
1 CONT While his image in most of the world is that of a ruthless bloodthirsty conqueror, Genghis Khan is an iconic and admired figure in Mongolia and China, where he is seen as the father of the Mongol Nation (see also Mongolia) and pre
1 CONC cursor to a united China under his grandson Kublai Khan. Before becoming a Khan, Temüjin eliminated as well as united the many nomadic tribes of north East Asia and Central Asia, and put them under a common social identity called 
1 CONC the "Mongol."
1 CONT
1 CONT Starting with the invasion of Western Xia and later Jin Dynasty (five dynasties) in northern China and consolidating through numerous conquests including the Khwarezmid Empire in Persia, Genghis Khan laid the foundation for an emp
1 CONC ire that was to leave an indelible mark on world history. Several centuries of Mongol rule across the Eurasian landmass, a period that some refer to as 'Pax Mongolica', radically altered the demography and geopolitics of these are
1 CONC as. The Mongol Empire ended up ruling, or at least briefly conquering, large parts of modern day China, Mongolia, Russia, Ukraine, Korea, Azerbaijan, Armenia, Georgia, Iraq, Iran, Turkey, Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Uzbekistan, Pakist
1 CONC an, Tajikistan, Afghanistan, Turkmenistan, Moldova, Kuwait, Poland and Hungary.
1 CONT
