0 @N6000000002187821025@ NOTE Euphrosyne Doukaina Kamatera
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Euphrosyne Doukaina Kamaterina or better Kamatera (Greek: ??f??s??? ????a??a ?aµate???a ? ?aµat???, Euphrosyne Doukaina Kamatera) (c. 1155 – 1211) was the wife of the Byzantine Emperor Alexios III Angelos.
1 CONT
1 CONT Euphrosyne was the daughter of Andronikos Doukas Kamateros, a high-ranking official who held the titles of megas droungarios and pansebastos (died 1176), and (accordingly to genealogist David Hughes) his second wife, as her second
1 CONC  husband, married in 1158, Qirwerne(je), sister of the 7th Zagwe Ethiopian Emperor Gebre Mesqel Lalibela (1119-1159)[1]. She was related to the Emperor Constantine X and Irene Doukaina, empress of Alexios I Komnenos. Both of her b
1 CONC rothers had rebelled against Andronikos I Komnenos; one was imprisoned and the other was blinded.
1 CONT
1 CONT Euphrosyne married Alexios Angelos, the older brother of the future Emperor Isaac II Angelos in c. 1169. Although Isaac II bestowed many titles and honors upon his brother, Alexios seized the throne on April 8, 1195, desposing Isa
1 CONC ac and proclaiming himself emperor. In this he was assisted by Euphrosyne, who had organized a party of aristocratic supporters. Euphrosyne took control of the palace and quelled the opposition herself, securing the accession of h
1 CONC er husband to the throne by wholesale bribery.
1 CONT
1 CONT Euphrosyne was a dominating woman with a talent for politics, and she virtually ruled the Empire in the name of Alexios III, who was concerned primarily with pleasure and idle pursuits. She issued commands herself and even altered
1 CONC  Alexios' decrees when it suited her. Euphrosyne and Alexios were criticized for their love of finery and the enrichment of their relatives at state expense. Her own brother, Basil Kamateros, and her son-in-law, Andronikos Kontost
1 CONC ephanos, accused Euphrosyne of adultery with one of her ministers, a nobleman named Vatatzes. Alexios III believed the allegations and had Vatatzes executed. Euphrosyne was stripped of her imperial robes and banished to a convent 
1 CONC at Nematarea in October 1196. However, her relatives convinced Alexios to reinstate her, and she was recalled six months later in spring 1197.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1203, faced with the Fourth Crusade and the return of his nephew, Alexios IV Angelos, Alexios III fled Constantinople with a magnificent treasure and some female relatives, including his daughter Eirene. Euphrosyne was left beh
1 CONC ind and was immediately imprisoned by the new regime. Alexios IV was soon strangled by Alexios Doukas Mourtzouphlos, the lover of Euphrosyne's daughter Eudokia, who then proclaimed himself emperor as Alexios V. In April 1204 Euphr
1 CONC osyne fled the city along with her daughter and Alexios V, and they made their way to Mosynoupolis, where Euphrosyne's husband Alexios III had taken refuge. Alexios III had Alexios V blinded and abandoned to the crusaders, who had
1 CONC  him executed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Euphrosyne and Alexios III fled across Greece to Thessalonica and Corinth, but were finally captured by Boniface of Montferrat and imprisoned. In 1209 or 1210 they were ransomed by their cousin Michael I of Epirus, and Euphrosyne 
1 CONC spent the remainder of her life in Arta. She died in 1210 or 1211.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT By her husband, Alexios III Angelos, Euphrosyne had three daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT Eirene Angelina, who married (1) Andronikos Kontostephanos; (2) Alexios Palaiologos, by whom she was the grandmother of Emperor Michael VIII Palaiologos. 
1 CONT Anna Angelina, who married (1) the sebastokrator Isaac Komnenos, great-nephew of Emperor Manuel I Komnenos; (2) Emperor Theodore I Laskaris of Nicaea. 
1 CONT Eudokia Angelina, who married (1) King Stefan I Prvovencani of Serbia; (2) Emperor Alexios V Doukas; (3) Leo Sgouros, ruler of Corinth. 
1 CONT Empress Euphrosyne Doukaina Kamatera's direct matrilineal 27th-generation descendant Grand Duchess Olga Constantinovna of Russia (1851-1926) became in 1867 the Queen-Consort of the Hellenes by marrying King George I of Greece. Que
1 CONC en Olga later, in 1920, became also the Regent of their kingdom, Greece.
1 CONT
1 CONT Empress Euphrosyne's matrilineal descent is, exceptionally, a verifiable one. There are still matrilineal descendants alive. Highly exceptional in longevity, her matrilineal descent has lasted now more than 800 years.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT The Oxford Dictionary of Byzantium, Oxford University Press, 1991 
1 CONT Garland, Lynda. Byzantine Empresses, 1999 
1 CONT Herrin, Judith (2001). Women in Purple:Rulers of Medieval Byzantium. London: Phoenix Press. ISBN 1-84212-529-X.  
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ The most famous Zagwe Emperor of Ethiopia was Lalibela. The "History of the Patriarchs", which usually just refers to the kings anonymously, calls him, "Lalibala son of Shanuda ("the Lion"), of the race of al-Nakba". Other sourc
1 CONC es add his throne-name, Gabra Masqal, and an epithet, "_be'esi 'azzal_", "the strong man". There is a story that the Ethiopian Emperor Lalibela, who, accompanied by his "troublesome" sister, Qirwerne, traveled to the Holy Land and
1 CONC  visited the Byzantine Emperor at Constantinople. There at the imperial court he and his sister may have met Izyaslav II of Kiev who was there visiting the emperor during the time of their visit. There are also undocumented legend
1 CONC s about him and his sister that probably are based on actual events. Everyone of these without exception says that his sister remained at the imperial court at Constantinople after Lalibela returned to Ethiopia. quote: Where, acco
1 CONC rding to Ethiopian history, he had to put down an uprising, or attempted _coup d'état_, spawned by his brother and sister. This is purportedly because of his contact with Lalibela, on pilgrimage. I surmise that it is possible that
1 CONC  Lalibela put down his brother and sister, before he went on the pilgrimage. I could not find a definite date for either. Going far from his nation for what should have been a protracted time, he took his rebellious sister with hi
1 CONC m, in order to keep an eye on her; that the rebel sister was none other than Qirwerne. If this were the case, what would have been more natural than for him to leave her in Constantinople, out of troubles' way? Meantime, the Ethio
1 CONC pian Princess married twice: once (in 1153) to Izyaslav II of Kiev (d1154) to whom she bore his posthumous daughter, Euphrosyne; according to Philipp Strahl's "Geschichte des Russischen Staates", 3 vols. (1866), and, upon returnin
1 CONC g to Constantinople after her first husband's death, Qirwerne married secondly (in 1158) to Andronikos Dukas Kamateros (d1176), by whom she was the mother of Euphrosyne (d1211), wife/empress of the Byzantine Emperor Alexius III (d
1 CONC 1210), which gives a "gateway" from Africa to Europe (note: the story about the involvement of an un-named widow of an un-named king and Andronikos Kamateros (reminds one of the story of the sister of England's King Henry VIII, na
1 CONC mely, Princess/Queen Mary, widow of King Louis XII of France, and her subsequent involvement and marriage to Charles Brandon) is the basis for the identification of the second husband of the Ethiopian princess, for circumstantial 
1 CONC evidence clearly identifies this un-named widow to have been Lalibela's "troublesome" sister, Qirwerne, and the un-named king to have been Izyaslav II, her first husband who died shortly after their marriage.) 
1 CONT Euphrosyne Doukaina Kamatera
1 CONT Doukas
1 CONT Born: c. 1155 1211 
