0 @N6000000000337130833@ NOTE (Research):Amadeus VI Encyclop‘dia Britannica Article    born 1334, Chamb‚ry, Savoy [now in France] died March 1, 1383, Castropignano [Italy]   byname Amadeus The Green Count,  Italian Amedeo Il Conte Verde, French Am‚d‚e Le Comte
1 CONC  Vert count of Savoy (1343-83) who significantly extended Savoy's territory and power.   Son of Aimone the Peaceful, count of Savoy, Amadeus ascended the throne at the age of nine. He crossed the Alps in 1348 to put down a revolt 
1 CONC of Piedmontese cities and won a victory over rebellious inhabitants of the Valais (east of Geneva) in 1352. In 1355 he married Bonne of Bourbon, sister-in-law of the dauphin Charles (later Charles V of France).   Military victorie
1 CONC s and purchases in the Alpine region in the 1350s increased his holdings, creating the geographic basis for a unified state comprising nearly the whole of the western Alps. He then acquired lands on the Italian side of the mountai
1 CONC ns.   In 1365 the Holy Roman emperor Charles IV made him imperial vicar. A year later Amadeus responded to Pope Urban V's call for a crusade against the Turks, whom he fought at Gallipoli. Following a campaign against the Bulgaria
1 CONC ns in the Black Sea area, he restored his cousin, John V Palaeologus, to the Byzantine throne.   Growing prestige made him the arbiter of many of the quarrels between the Italian powers. He acted as mediator between Pisa and Flore
1 CONC nce in 1364, between the marquesses of Montferrat and the Visconti family from 1375 to 1379, and between Venice and Milan on the one hand and Genoa on the other, negotiating the Peace of Turin (1381). The following year he brought
1 CONC  about an accord between the Genoese and the king of Cyprus and was later invited by the Guelf (pro-papal) nobles of Genoa to become doge and take the city under his protection_an empty offer, since the Guelfs were out of power. I
1 CONC n 1382 Amadeus joined Pope Clement VII and the French prince Louis of Anjou in the project of rescuing Queen Joan I of Naples from the rival pope Urban VI and Charles of Durazzo, pretender to the throne of Naples. Launched in June
1 CONC  1382, the expedition was beset by sickness and hunger and bogged down in southern Italy. The following winter Amadeus was stricken with plague and died.
0 @N6000000002188625313@ NOTE Stefan Uroš III Decanski of Serbia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Stefan of Decani
1 CONT King of Serbia
1 CONT
1 CONT King Stefan Uroš III Decanski
1 CONT Reign 1321 - 1331
1 CONT Coronation 1321
1 CONT Born 1285
1 CONT Died November 11, 1331 (aged 46)
1 CONT Place of death Castle of Zvecan
1 CONT Predecessor Stefan Uroš II Milutin
1 CONT Successor Dušan the Mighty
1 CONT Consort Theodora of Bulgaria, Maria Palaiologina
1 CONT Issue Stefan Uroš IV Dušan, Simeon Uroš
1 CONT Royal House House of Nemanjic
1 CONT Father Stefan Uroš II Milutin
1 CONT Mother Anna of Bulgaria
1 CONT
1 CONT Stefan Uroš III Decanski (Serbian: ?????? ???? III ????????), (c. 1285 – November 11, 1331) reigned as king of Serbia from 1321 to September 8, 1331. He took his name from the great monastery he built at Decani.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of King Stefan Uroš II and Anna of Bulgaria. His maternal grandparents were George I of Bulgaria and his wife Maria, sister of Ivan Asen III of Bulgaria.
1 CONT
1 CONT While still a youth, he was sent by his father as a hostage to Nogai Khan of the Golden Horde, and on his return was entrusted with the governorship of Zeta (i.e., Montenegro).
1 CONT
1 CONT He was married (first) to Theodora, daughter of Smilets of Bulgaria. He was secondly married to Byzantine princess Maria Palaiologina.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1314 his father, quarrelling with Stefan, sent him to Constantinople to have him blinded there. Stefen was never blinded properly but had to wear black bandage over his eyes. In 1320 he was permitted to return to Serbia and had
1 CONC  to defeat several pretenders to the throne before being crowned in 1321. These included his half-brother Stefan Constantine, whom he defeated and killed in 1322, and his cousin Stefan Vladislav II, whom he defeated and exiled in 
1 CONC 1324.
1 CONT Coins minted by Stefan Decanski
1 CONT Coins minted by Stefan Decanski
1 CONT
1 CONT The claimants enjoyed foreign support, and the victorious king was faced by the alliance of Bulgaria and the Byzantine Empire. Michael Asen III of Bulgaria divorced Stefan Uroš III's sister Anna and married the Byzantine princess 
1 CONC Theodora Palaiologina instead. The allies intended to join forces for a major invasion of Serbia in 1330. This led to the most significant event of Stefan Uroš III's reign, the Battle of Velbuzhd, in which he defeated the Bulgaria
1 CONC ns and killed Bulgarian emperor Michael Asen III.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hearing of his ally's defeat, the Byzantine Emperor Andronikos III Palaiologos retreated, turning to easier prey. His conquests allowed him to push the Serbian borders to the south into Byzantine Macedonia. Some of his courtiers, 
1 CONC however, were discontented with his policies and conspired to dethrone him in favour of his son Dušan. The latter imprisoned Stefan Uroš III in the castle of Zvecan, where he presently died a violent death by strangulation.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Stefan's actions were frequently far from saintly, the Serbian Orthodox Church had him canonized. His remains are venerated at the largest medieval church of Serbia that he built at Decani. His feast day is November 24.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Ancestors
1 CONT Stefan Uroš's Ancestos in three generations Stefan Uroš III Decanski, King of Serbia Father:
1 CONT Stefan Uroš II Milutin Paternal Grandfather:
1 CONT Stefan Uroš I, King of Serbia Paternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Stefan Prvovencani, King of Serbia
1 CONT Paternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Anne Dandolo, Queen of Serbia
1 CONT Paternal Grandmother:
1 CONT Helene d'Anjou Paternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Paternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Mother:
1 CONT Anna Terter Maternal Grandfather:
1 CONT George Terter I, Tsar of Bulgaria Maternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Maternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Maternal Grandmother:
1 CONT Kira Maria of Bulgaria Maternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Mitso Asen Tsar of Bulgaria
1 CONT Maternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Maria of Bulgaria
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT By his first wife, Theodora of Bulgaria, Stefan Uroš III had several children, including:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Stefan Uroš IV Dušan, who succeeded as king
1 CONT     * Helena (Jelena), who married Mladen III Šubic
1 CONT     * Theodora, who married sebastokrator Dejan
1 CONT
1 CONT By his second wife, Maria Palaiologina, Stefan Uroš III had:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Simeon Uroš Palaiologos
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Stefan Uroš II Milutin King of Serbia
1 CONT 1321–1331 Succeeded by
1 CONT Stefan Uroš IV Dušan
0 @N6000000002188625353@ NOTE Simeon Uroš
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Simeon Uroš or Siniša Uroš, also Symeon Ouresis Palaiologos (Serbian Cyrillic: ?????? ????; Greek: S?µe?? ???es?? ?a?a???????, Symeon Oureses Palaiologos), was the ruler of Epirus from 1359 to 1366 and of Thessaly from 1359 until 
1 CONC his death in c. 1370.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT
1 CONT Simeon Uroš, nicknamed Siniša, was the son of King Stefan Uroš III Decanski of Serbia by his second wife, Maria Palaiologina, the daughter of the despotes John Palaiologos, a grandson of Emperor Michael VIII Palaiologos.
1 CONT
1 CONT When his half-brother Emperor Stefan Uroš IV Dušan conquered Epirus and Acarnania in 1348, he appointed as governor of these regions Simeon Uroš, whom he had granted the title of despotes traditionally reserved for emperors' broth
1 CONC ers and younger sons. Simeon Uroš consolidated his position in relation to the local aristocracy by marrying Thomais, the daughter of the former ruler of Epirus, John Orsini.
1 CONT
1 CONT Simeon Uroš's relatively uneventful governorship was interrupted when, shortly after Dušan's death in 1355, his brother-in-law Nikephoros II Orsini, the deposed ruler of Epirus, reappeared in Greece and gained the support of the n
1 CONC obility in Thessaly and Epirus. In 1356 Nikephoros entered Epirus and forced Simeon Uroš to flee to Kastoria, where he attempted to proclaim himself emperor of Serbs and Greeks in the place of his nephew Stefan Uroš V. Although he
1 CONC  gained the support of some important magnates like John Komnenos Asen (the brother of Dušan's widow), Simeon Uroš was unable to assert himself against the wishes of the nobility of Serbia proper and Macedonia.
1 CONT
1 CONT After he was forced to retreat from his attempt to invade Zeta (Montenegro) in 1358, Simeon Uroš gave up hope of asserting himself in Serbia. The next year, however, Nikephoros II Orsini was killed in a skirmish against the Albani
1 CONC ans, and opened up a welcome opportunity for Simeon Uroš. Consequently, he rapidly swept into Thessaly and was acknowledged as its ruler in 1359. He then invaded Epirus, where the towns, harried by the Albanian clansmen who had ta
1 CONC ken over the countryside, also recognized his authority.
1 CONT
1 CONT While Simeon Uroš was in Epirus, Radoslav Hlapen of Vodena attempted to seize Thessaly on behalf of his stepson Thomas Preljubovic. Simeon Uroš was forced to cut his losses by recognizing Radoslav Hlapen's conquests, turning over 
1 CONC Kastoria to him, and marrying his daughter Maria to Thomas. Hlapen recognized Simeon Uroš's suzerainty in at least some of these lands and provided a buffer between him and the Serbian nobles to the north. Simeon Uroš established 
1 CONC himself in Trikkala in Thessaly, and spent the remaining decade of his reign in relative peace. The effectiveness of his rule over Epirus was curtailed, however, by the Albanian clans, and he was forced to recognize two of their l
1 CONC eaders, John Bova Spata and Peter Liosha, as his vassals in Arta and Angelokastron. In 1366 he turned over Ioannina, his last major possession in Epirus, to his son-in-law Thomas, who reigned there as vassal despotes.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Trikala Simeon Uroš presided over a court including Byzantine, Serbian, and Albanian nobles, but he showed preference for the Byzantine relatives of his wife. He also founded and generously endowed the monasteries of Meteora. H
1 CONC e died sometime between 1369 and 1371.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT By his marriage to Thomais Orsini, Simeon Uroš had three children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * John Uroš, who succeeded as ruler of Thessaly.
1 CONT     * Stephen Uroš, governor of Pharsalos.
1 CONT     * Maria, who married Thomas II Preljubovic, who succeeded as ruler of Epirus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Nikephoros II Ruler of Epirus
1 CONT 1359–1366 Succeeded by
1 CONT Thomas II
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Nikephoros II Ruler of Thessaly
1 CONT 1359–1370 Succeeded by
1 CONT John Uroš
